Chapter 5: 

1. Using Files for Data Storage

Files are used to save data for later use.

There are three steps:

1. Open the file.
2. Process the file (Read or Write).
3. Close the file.

There are two types of files:

- Text File: Stores data as text.
- Binary File: Stores data in binary format.

2. File Accessing

C# provides two important classes:

- StreamWriter: Used to write data into a text file.
- StreamReader: Used to read data from a text file.

3. Writing Data to a File

We use StreamWriter to write information into a file.

Example:

StreamWriter outputFile;
outputFile = File.CreateText("courses.txt");

outputFile.WriteLine("Java");
outputFile.WriteLine("C#");
outputFile.WriteLine("Python");

outputFile.Close();

- "CreateText()" creates a new file or replaces existing content.
- "WriteLine()" writes data and moves to a new line.
- "Write()" writes data without moving to a new line.
- "Close()" closes the file.

4. CreateText vs AppendText

- CreateText(): Creates a file and removes old content if the file already exists.
- AppendText(): Adds new data without deleting existing content.

Example:

StreamWriter outputFile;
outputFile = File.AppendText("Names.txt");

outputFile.WriteLine("Ali");
outputFile.WriteLine("Ahmed");

outputFile.Close();

5. Reading Data from a File

We use StreamReader to read information from a file.

Important methods:

- "ReadLine()" reads one line.
- "Read()" reads characters.
- "ReadToEnd()" reads all remaining content.
- "Close()" closes the file.

Example:

StreamReader inputFile;
inputFile = File.OpenText("students.txt");

string line = inputFile.ReadLine();

listBox1.Items.Add(line);

inputFile.Close();

6. Reading a File with a Loop

We use "EndOfStream" to check whether the file has reached the end.

Example:

StreamReader inputFile;
inputFile = File.OpenText("students.txt");

while (!inputFile.EndOfStream)
{
    listBox1.Items.Add(inputFile.ReadLine());
}

inputFile.Close();

7. OpenFileDialog and SaveFileDialog

These controls display Windows dialog boxes.

- OpenFileDialog: Allows the user to select and open a file.
- SaveFileDialog: Allows the user to choose where to save a file.

They are invisible controls and appear in the Component Tray.

8. ShowDialog() and DialogResult

- "ShowDialog()" displays the dialog box.
- "DialogResult.OK" means the user clicked OK.
- "DialogResult.Cancel" means the user clicked Cancel.

Example:

if (openFileDialog.ShowDialog() == DialogResult.OK)
{
    MessageBox.Show("File selected");
}
else
{
    MessageBox.Show("Cancelled");
}

9. Filename and InitialDirectory

- Filename: Gets the selected file path and filename.
- InitialDirectory: Specifies the folder displayed first.

Example:

openFileDialog.InitialDirectory = @"C:\Data";

if (openFileDialog.ShowDialog() == DialogResult.OK)
{
    StreamReader inputFile =
        File.OpenText(openFileDialog.FileName);

    inputFile.Close();
}